  "worked_solution": "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}=2.1x(1-0.025x)-1.1xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $2.1x(1-0.025x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-1.1xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}=2.1x(1-0.025x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.1x(1-0.025x)=0\\,\u21d2\\,x=0,\\,40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=40.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $40.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
